Excellent. Let's knock out **Bug 3: The Distributed Scheduler Race Condition**.
### The Problem
In your HdfsRecoveryService, you have the @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 300000) annotation. In a production environment with multiple instances (pods) of your JournalService running behind a load balancer, every single instance will trigger this method at the same time.
They will all query the HDFS_SYNC_RETRY_QUEUE for PENDING records, pull the exact same batch IDs, and concurrently fire hdfsSyncService.syncToDataLake(). This race condition will blast the Data Lake with duplicate update commands.
### The Fix
Since your architecture already relies heavily on Redis, we will implement a distributed lock using StringRedisTemplate. We'll use the "Set If Absent" (SETNX) command.
When the scheduler fires, the instance will try to place a lock in Redis. Only the *first* instance to reach Redis will acquire the lock and process the queue. The other instances will see the lock exists and quietly skip their execution for that cycle.
